{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3428","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3428","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3428\",\"parent_revision\":\"80442441c159ba2702b357c90b0e017c5e9851cd510cf152211e257a7c23c62c\",\"root_revision\":\"80442441c159ba2702b357c90b0e017c5e9851cd510cf152211e257a7c23c62c\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEach of the 19 is a link from a plugin file to something outside that plugin \u2014 frequently into `research/`, `docs/`, or a sibling plugin \u2014 that will 404 for anyone who installs that plugin standalone from the marketplace.\\n\\nThe detection gap and the violations compound: the check exists, is generic, and would find all 19, but it is exercised against one plugin. So the invariant reads as enforced while five plugins carry breakage.\\n</div>\\n\\n## What And Why\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPR #3422 added `tests/test_plugin_self_containment.py`, which walks a plugin directory and fails on any markdown link that resolves outside it \u2014 the class of bug that breaks a plugin when installed standalone in another repo (a link into this monorepo's own `rules/`, `docs/`, etc.). The check function (`find_self_containment_violations`) is written generically over any plugin directory, but the one test in that PR hardcodes it to `plugins/agent-orchestration` only, since that's the plugin the PR was actually rewriting.\\n\\nAn adversarial review of PR #3422 (`code-review xhigh`, altitude angle) ran the same generic function against every directory under `plugins/` in the post-merge worktree and found **19 real violations across 5 other plugins**, currently undetected by CI:\\n\\n- `development-harness`: 7\\n- `plugin-creator`: 7\\n- `python3-development`: 1\\n- `scientific-method`: 3\\n- `the-rewrite-room`: 1\\n\\nExample: `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md:11` links `../../../research/README.md`, which escapes the plugin root \u2014 confirmed directly (not just claimed) by running the check function against that file.\\n\\nAlso confirmed directly: `skilllint`'s NR002 rule (\\\"Namespace reference points outside plugin directory\\\"), despite the name suggesting overlap, does **not** catch this class of link \u2014 it did not fire against the known-bad DH link above, and `skilllint check` doesn't even scan plain `docs/*.md` files (only `SKILL.md` and similar). So this hand-rolled test is not redundant with existing tooling; it's the only thing catching this defect class.\\n</div>\\n\\n## What Must Remain True\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `find_self_containment_violations()`'s existing generic contract (works against any plugin directory) stays unchanged.\\n- Once fixed, `tests/test_plugin_self_containment.py` should be parametrized over every directory in `plugins/*` rather than hardcoded to `plugins/agent-orchestration`, so the invariant is enforced repo-wide going forward.\\n- The 19 existing violations need triage: each one is a link from a plugin file to something outside that plugin (frequently into `research/`, `docs/`, or a sibling plugin) that will 404 for anyone who installs that plugin standalone from the marketplace. Each needs either an in-plugin replacement (mirroring what PR #3422 did for `agent-orchestration`'s two outward links) or, if the reference is genuinely meant to be repo-internal-only content, an explicit decision that the affected file is design-time/authoring-only and should be excluded from the check.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Not In Scope For The Pr That Filed This\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFixing this was correctly deferred rather than expanding PR #3422 (a delegation-system consolidation) into a 5-plugin cross-cutting fix.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"c5629a7008a94a53236172a53e08d9fca0e7e0df5d4ef25af44724ba0fa909f1\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABTSBfoQ\"}"}